Notebook[{
Cell[TextData[StyleBox["INTRODUCTION:\nNumerical v.s. Symbolic\nComputing\n\n\
Monday, 1 August 2005",
  FontSize->24,
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Title"],

Cell["", "Subtitle"],

Cell["A. Numerical Computation", "Subtitle"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "With some exceptions, most programming languages are designed for \
numerical computing, e.g., Fortran, C, and Matlab packages. The most obvious \
limitations are (1) limited range of integers, (2) finite accuracy of \
floating point numbers.",
  StyleBox["\n", "Subtitle"]
}], "Text"],

Cell["B. Symbolic Computing", "Subtitle"],

Cell["\<\
Symbolic computing environment overcomes the two limitations \
(arbitrary sizes for integers, and a user-specified accuracy in floating \
point calculation).   Most importantly, symbolic packages provided ability of \
manipulating formulas, e.g., differentiation or integration of a function.  \
The disadvantage of symbolic systems is that it is typically slower.
\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["Symbolic Packages (Languages)", "Subtitle"],

Cell["\<\
(1)  LISP:  low level but powerful programming language.
(2)  Maple, Mathematica:  two popular symbolic computing packages.
(3)  and many other special packages: reduce, axiom, macsyma, etc.
\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["How to run Mathematica",
  FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]]], "Subtitle"],

Cell["A. Text-Based Interfaces", "Subtitle"],

Cell["Log into your UNIX system, type", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(math\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
You are engaged in a dialog with the system, one line at time, RETURN to \
enter an input.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["How to run Mathematica",
    FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],
  "\nB. Notebook Interfaces (GUI)"
}], "Subtitle"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Log into your Window system (Start button, All Programs, ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "),  click"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(mathematica\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "You can access the help as well as the whole book \"",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ": a system for doing Mathematics by Computer\" by S. Wolfram."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    RowBox[{\(IMPORTANT : \ You\ execute\ an\ Mathematica\ Expression\ with\),
       " "}], "\n", 
    RowBox[{"\t\t", 
      StyleBox[\(SHIFT - ENTER\),
        FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]]}]}], "Input"],

Cell["Arbitrary Large Integers", "Subtitle"],

Cell["Simple integer calculations", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(2 + 2\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(2^90\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    RowBox[{
      StyleBox["+",
        FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]], 
      RowBox[{",", " ", 
        RowBox[{
          StyleBox["-",
            FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]], 
          RowBox[{",", " ", 
            RowBox[{
              StyleBox["*",
                FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]], 
              RowBox[{",", " ", 
                RowBox[{
                  StyleBox["/",
                    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]], 
                  RowBox[{",", " ", 
                    RowBox[{
                      RowBox[{
                        StyleBox["^",
                          FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]], " ", "\n", "for"}], 
                      " ", "add"}], ",", "subtract", ",", "multiply", ",", 
                    " ", "divide", ",", \(and\ power\), 
                    ",", \(as\ \(\(usual\)\(.\)\)\)}]}]}]}]}]}]}]}]], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Integer functions (GCD[a,b], greatest common divisor, FactorInteger[a], etc):\
\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(GCD[10, 4]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(FactorInteger[40]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This means 40 = ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`2\^3\)]],
  " 5."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    StyleBox[\(Function\ arguments\ are\ enclosed\ in\ square\ \(\(brakets\ [\
\  ... \ ]\)\(.\)\)\),
      FontColor->GrayLevel[0.500008]]], "Input"],

Cell["Floating Point with Arbitrary Precision", "Subtitle"],

Cell["\<\
The function N[...] converts an exact number into floating point. \
(N[ ] does not increase the precision of a number).\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(N[2^90]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(N[Pi, 25]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(1/3\  + \ 2/7\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(N[1/3\  + \ 2/7]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Mathematica's rules 

for numerical calculations:
    \t(1): If input is exact, the computation will be performed exactly.
    \t(2): If input is approximate, the result is approximate, with accuracy \
not great than the input.\
\>", "Theorem",
  FontSize->14,
  FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->0}],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(452/62\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(452.3/62\)], "Input"],

Cell["Why is there a difference in the two results?", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Precision[1.34]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \($MachinePrecision\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Precision[123.44444444444444444]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Two kinds of approximate real nos:
(1): Arbitrary precision numbers contains any number of digits.
(2): Machine precision numbers contain fixed number of digits.
If less than $MachinePrecision digits, number treated as (2) else as \
(1)\
\>", "Theorem",
  FontSize->14,
  FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->0}],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Accuracy[134.01]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Precision[x] : total no. of significant digits in x
Accuracy[x] : no. of significant digits to right of decimal point in x\
\>", \
"Theorem",
  FontSize->14,
  FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->0}],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(x\  = \ SetPrecision[1.3, \ 28]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Precision[x]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(y\  = \ x\ *\ 1.0; \ Precision[y]\)], "Input"],

Cell["Why x has 28 digits of precision but y is 16?", "Text"],

Cell["Symbol Data Type", "Subtitle"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Like any programming language, ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " is provided with the usual data type of integer, floating point numbers \
and advanced structures. In addition, ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " provides a type called \"Symbol\" for symbolic computation.  For \
example:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(x\  + \ 3  x\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
The function Head[...] tells you what data type a particular \
expression is.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Head[x]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Head[3]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Head[3\  + \ I\ ]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Head[2.0]\)], "Input"],

Cell["Some Examples of Symbolic Computation", "Subtitle"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Expand[\ \((1 + x)\)^2]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor[\ 1\  + \ 4\ x\  + \ 3  x^2]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor[x^99 + y^99]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Solve[x^2 + 2*x + 1\  \[Equal] \ 0]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Solve[\ {y^2\  + \ x\  - y\  == 0, \ 
        y\  - \ x^2\ \  + 2 \[Equal] \ 0}, \ {x, y}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Solve[a*x^2 + b*x + c\  \[Equal] \ 0, \ x]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(D[Sin[x], x]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(D[x^n, \ {x, \ 2}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Integrate\ [\ e^{x^2}, \ x]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Integrate[\ \((1 + x)\)^3/\((1 - x^2)\)^2, \ x]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Simplify[Sin[x]^2\  + \ Cos[x]^2]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\

Graphical Computations
\
\>", "Subtitle"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " supports graphics in a convenient way, as well as more advanced \
programming environment.  A simple plot :"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Plot[\ Sin[x], \ {x, \ \(-Pi\), \ Pi}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Plot3D[\ 
      Exp[\(-\((x^2 + y^2)\)\)], \ {x, \ \(-2\), 2}, \ {y, \ \(-2\), \ 
        2}]\)], "Input"],

Cell["DEFINING FUNCTIONS", "Title"],

Cell["Simple One-Line Function Definition", "Subtitle"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f[x_]\  := \ x^2\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
This defines a function that takes the square of its argument.   x_ \
(the symbol called x with an underscore) is called a pattern (which \"matches\
\" anything), and := is delayed assignment, different from assignment =.  We \
use the function like this:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f[2]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f[a]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f[1 + x]\)], "Input"],

Cell["The function takes any argument, including strings:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f["\<SMA\>"]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Here f[x_] := x^2 specifies value to set of array elements with \
arbitrary indices while f[x] := x^2 specifies value to an element of array.\
\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(g[x]\  := \ x^2\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(g[x]\  + \ g[y]\)], "Input"],

Cell["Assignment = and Delayed Assignment :=", "Subtitle"],

Cell["\<\
= (or Set[ ])  The right-hand side expression is evaluated, and \
result sets to the variable (expression) on the left-hand side.
:= (or SetDelayed[ ]) The right-hand side expression without evaluation is \
set to the variable (expression) on the left-hand side.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(a\  = \ 3; \ x\  = \ a + 1\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(?x\)\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
With \"=\", we associate number 4 with variable x, since a + 1 has \
been evaluated to 4 before setting the value for x.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(a\  = \ 3; \ x\  := \ a\  + \ 1\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(?x\)\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
With \":=\", we associate a + 1 with x, the expression a+1 is \
delayed for evaluation. \
\>", "Text"],

Cell["More on Function Definition", "Subtitle"],

Cell["\<\
If we want a function to take only integer arguments, we can say:\
\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(g[z_Integer]\  := \ z^2\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({g[3], \ g[x], \ g[3.5], \ g[p]}\)], "Input"],

Cell["We can define a function with any number of arguments:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(h[a_, \ b_, \ c_]\  := \ \((a + b + c)\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(h[1, 2, q]\)], "Input"],

Cell["You can define a function with variable number of arguments:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(v[a_]\  := \ Plus[a]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({v[1], \ v[1, 3], \ v[1, 2, 3, 4]}\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(v[a__]\  := \ Plus[a]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({v[1], \ v[1, 3], \ v[1, 2, 3, 4]}\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Function Definition with More than One-Line of Code: Programs\
\>", \
"Subtitle"],

Cell["\<\
If we define a function with a code of more than one line, we can \
used the Module[ ] to group the code as a single unit.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(\(prog[
        x_]\)\(\ \)\(:=\)\(\ \)\(Module[\ {a, b, c}, \ \n\t\t\ta\  = \ 
          1; \n\t\t\tb\  = \ 2; \n\t\t\tc\  = \ a\  + \ b; \n\t\t\tx\  + \ 
          c]\)\(\t\)\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(prog[z]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
The variables in the first list {a,b,c} specifies LOCAL variables \
to this function. Statements are separated by semicolon \";\".   The return \
value of the function is last expression, x + c, without an ending semicolon. \
The argument x must be treated as a constant, rather than a variable (i.e., \
you cannot do x = a + b).\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["Patterns", "Subtitle"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Pattern is an important concept in ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "'s syntax:  f[x_] is a pattern.  Patterns are used for matching an \
expression.  So f[x_] matched any expression with an explicit f, followed by \
square brackets with x_ being replaced by any expression.  So f[x_] matches \
f[1], f[x], or f[\"this\"], but not g[2].   If pattern matches, the \
definition associated with that pattern is executed.   In the above example, \
f[something] is replaced by something^2.  \n\ng[x_Integer]  matches a \
function g with integer argument, so, it matches g[2], but not g[a] (assuming \
we did not say a = 3, so a is not an integer).  Unmatched patterns are not \
executed and returned the same as input.   h[a_, b_, c_] matches function h \
with three arbitrary arguments.\n\nv[a__] (two underscores) matches function \
v with 1 or more arguments.  So it matches v[1], v[1,2],  or v[a,b,c].\nYou \
can test if pattern ABC matches XYZ by the command:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(MatchQ[ABC, \ XYZ]\)], "Input"],

Cell["For example,", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(MatchQ[f[y], \ f[x_]]\)], "Input"],

Cell["Substitution Operations", "Title"],

Cell["\<\
A convenient way for evaluation of an expression is by variable \
substitution. \
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((1 + x)\)^5\ \ \  /. \ x -> \ 3\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
While x = 3 globally sets x to 3, x -> 3 replaces x by 3 only \
locally at the current expression. \
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({Sin[x], Cos[x], Exp[x]} /. x -> 0\)], "Input"],

Cell["Expression of the form x -> 0 is called a rule,.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((x + y)\) \((x - y)\)^2 /. {x \[Rule] \ 3, \ 
        y \[Rule] \ 1 - a}\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Prefix, postfix, and infix notation 
for function application.\
\>", "Subtitle"],

Cell["We normally say Sin[x], but we could say:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Sin\ @\ Pi\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Pi\  // \ Sin\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Join[{a, b, c}, {d, e}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({a, b, c}~\ Join\ ~{d, e}\)], "Input"]
},
FrontEndVersion->"5.1 for Microsoft Windows",
ScreenRectangle->{{0, 1024}, {0, 689}},
WindowSize->{1020, 655},
WindowMargins->{{Automatic, 0}, {Automatic, 0}},
ShowSelection->True,
Magnification->3,
StyleDefinitions -> "Classroom.nb"
]

